window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][105] = "<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> The studies measure different things in different populations; the text-recall study used short-term laboratory material, the meta-analysis used observed correlations across domains with varied definitions; neither studied job interviews.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported: the design and results of the testing study (the five-minute reversal, the one-week figures, the confidence finding); the meta-analysis percentages by domain; the dispute about the definition of deliberate practice and the reanalysis figure.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> A candidate must decide how to spend limited preparation time: rereading and watching, or timed, scored retrieval with feedback; and how much weight to give hours. As an observable decision: &quot;What does the evidence say about how to practice, and how far does it apply?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis, through this chapter's tools.</em></p>\n<p><em>Retrieval beats rereading for lasting learning.</em> The testing study shows that practice that requires producing the material produces better retention a week later than rereading, even though rereading produces better performance at five minutes and more confidence. The implication for preparation (C34.1): do mocks, not rereads; judge progress by delayed, realistic tests, not by how fluent the chapter felt. <em>Caution:</em> the material was prose passages, not complex skills like structuring a product answer, and the tests had no feedback; interview skills involve more than recall.</p>\n<p><em>Confidence is not evidence.</em> The finding that repeated study raised confidence while lowering retention is a reason for calibration (C34.4): your feeling of readiness is a poor guide, and outside scores are better.</p>\n<p><em>Hours matter, but not alone.</em> The meta-analysis shows that accumulated practice hours explain only part of the differences in performance, and much less in professional and educational settings; the disagreement is about what counts as deliberate practice. Both sides agree that practice matters and that its quality matters. For preparation: do not use hours as the target (Goodhart); use structured practice aimed at a specific weakness, with feedback and repetition (C34.7), and track results.</p>\n<p><em>Spacing.</em> The testing study's delayed tests, and the broader literature, measured retention a week after learning, but this study did not vary spacing itself; the case relies on the wider literature on spaced retesting, which was not read here, for that part, and the 72-hour retest in the loop is a design choice by the author.</p>\n<p><em>What the evidence does not show.</em> It does not show how many mocks produce an offer, whether retrieval practice transfers from text recall to spoken interviews at the same size, or whether the effect sizes carry to your situation. The numbers are from different domains and must not be applied as a forecast. The best use of the evidence is as support for a design principle, tested by your own scores.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a candidate could face.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Reread chapters and watch samples; log hours</td>\n<td>Comfort and confidence</td>\n<td>Weaker retention and skill; false confidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Timed mocks and drills with scorecards, calibration and spaced retests</td>\n<td>Delayed performance; measured gaps</td>\n<td>Effort, partner time, uncomfortable scores</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Maximize hours of any practice</td>\n<td>Volume</td>\n<td>Quality varies; hours alone explain little</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B is effortful and aligned with how retention and skill appear to develop; A feels better; C ignores quality.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Use B as the default, with reading limited to learning methods before practicing them, and judge progress by calibrated scores on realistic, delayed tests, not by hours or confidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> The design of this chapter's practice system (mocks, scorecards, calibration, error log, spaced retest) follows from these findings. Whether it works for a given reader is tested by that reader's own score trends, which is why section 13 asks for them.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> If a reader found that rereading raised their mock scores more than drills, they should follow their data. A reader should also be cautious about quoting the study figures as if they applied to interviews. The method to carry away: practice by producing, with feedback, spaced over time; distrust fluency and hours as measures; and let outside scores decide.</p>\n<span id=\"c34-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Ten questions. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code> (composite candidates), and every figure is invented (invented). Each ends with follow-ups and answers. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round. Several are questions interviewers ask directly (feedback, pressure, readiness) and several are product, analytical and AI questions about practice itself, which test the same skills in a different setting.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c34-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C34-01 · Beginner · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you structure your practice for the month before a loop?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd practice as many questions as I can.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A schedule of mocks, review and a taper.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Timed partner mocks with scorecards, calibration, an error log with one fix per session and retests, a data-driven target from the weakest dimension, and a protected final week.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd assume about eight hours a week and spend it on retrieval, not rereading. Week one: build the loop map with the recruiter (C33.1), read only to learn methods, and run three solo timed drills and one partner mock, scoring myself first with the scorecard and then comparing with the partner. Week two: two partner mocks, recorded, with an error log and one fix per session, retested within 72 hours; compute my bias against the partner. Week three: diagnose my weakest important dimension from the scores and put about half of my hours there with a numeric goal; one full mock loop at the end. Week four: taper. A last partner mock of one round type on day minus three, light review of story headlines and the company brief, logistics settled two days before, and rest the day before. I'd judge progress by calibrated scores by dimension on realistic mocks, not by hours logged.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Retrieval-based; scorecards; calibration; a data-driven target; a taper.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if you can't find a partner? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd use recorded solo mocks and score them against the scorecard, and I'd calibrate by scoring a recording of mine from a month ago, to check my consistency. I'd also swap with another candidate or ask a colleague in a product role for two sessions, since even a few outside scores correct a lot of bias.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if you only have three weeks? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd compress: skip the reading, do the first partner mock on day two to find the gaps, give most hours to the weakest dimension, and keep the taper. The order of importance is realistic mocks, scoring with evidence, one fix, a retest, rest.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c34-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C34-02 · Beginner · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How do you give useful feedback to a peer after a mock interview?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I tell them what I liked and what to improve.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Scores against a rubric with evidence and gives one priority fix.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Scores independently before discussing, quotes the evidence, separates strengths from fixes, keeps to one or two fixes, asks the peer to self-score first, and agrees a retest.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Before the mock I ask the peer what rubric we are using and what they want me to probe. During it I take notes as evidence statements, quoting what they said. At the end we each score independently before talking. Then I ask them to share their score first and the evidence for it, which makes them self-assess, and I compare: where we differ by two points or more, we look at the quote and discuss who had the better evidence.</p>\n<p>&quot;I give two strengths with evidence ('you named the user and the number in the first minute') and one or two fixes, not ten: 'You gave a metric without a denominator; try stating numerator and denominator.' I avoid dumping solutions: I say what I observed and the effect, and let them choose how to fix it. We agree one fix and a retest on a different prompt within three days. I also thank them, since honest feedback is uncomfortable to receive and easy to soften.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Independent scoring; evidence; priorities; a retest.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if your peer gets defensive? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd go back to the evidence: 'Here is the sentence you said; here is what I wrote.' I'd ask what they were trying to do, which often reveals a gap in what came across. If it still goes badly, I'd stop and offer to send written notes, so they can read them without an audience.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle being much better, or much weaker, than your partner? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A weaker partner needs fewer fixes and more confidence on strengths; a stronger one gives me more than I give, and I should ask for their scorecard habits. Either way I'd use the same rubric so that the feedback is about the answer and not about me.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c34-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C34-03 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Tell me about a time you received critical feedback.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I was told to improve my communication and I did.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the feedback, the change and a result.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows openness (asked for specifics), a tested change with numbers, an outside check of the fix, a lasting habit, and honesty about the initial reaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>(A composite story, shown as notes then told.)</em> <em>Notes.</em> Context: my first readout to senior stakeholders was a deck of 22 slides with charts and no recommendation; my manager said afterward, &quot;I didn't know what you wanted us to decide&quot; (invented). Decision: defend the work (it was thorough), or change the format. Action: I asked her for an example of a readout she liked; she sent a one-page memo with the recommendation first; I rewrote mine as a one-page decision document with the ask, options, recommendation and what would change my mind; I asked two colleagues outside the project to read it and say the point after one read; the first could not, so I cut a third and rewrote the opening. Result: the next readout took 20 minutes instead of 60, and the decision was made in the meeting; stakeholders approved the proposal in one session instead of two. Learning: the reader's time is the measure, and I now ask for feedback after each deliverable with a specific question: &quot;What would have made this easier to act on?&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Told (about 1 minute 10 seconds).</em> &quot;After my first readout to senior stakeholders, my manager told me she didn't know what I wanted them to decide. My deck had 22 slides and no recommendation, and my first reaction was that the thoroughness showed effort. I could defend it or change it. I asked her for an example she liked, which was a one-page memo with the recommendation first, and I rewrote mine in that form: the ask, the options, my recommendation and what would change my mind. I asked two colleagues to read it and tell me the point after one read; the first couldn't, so I cut a third and rewrote the opening. The next readout took 20 minutes instead of 60 and we got the decision in the room. What I took from it is that the reader's time is the measure, and I now ask for feedback on every major piece with one question: what would have made this easier to act on?&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Honesty about the first reaction; a tested change; an outside check; a lasting habit.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Was it hard to hear? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Yes: I had worked for weeks on it, and the feedback felt like a judgment on the effort. What helped was asking for an example, which turned a verdict into a model I could follow.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Have you received feedback you disagreed with? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Once, that I should be more decisive in meetings. I didn't agree entirely, since I wanted more input first; I tried a version of it: I state my recommendation first and ask for objections, which keeps the input and shows a view. I checked with her after a month and she said it landed better.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c34-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C34-04 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You did well in mock interviews but froze in the real one. What happened, and what would you change?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I was nervous; I'd stay calmer.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies differences between mocks and the real round and plans practice.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Diagnoses transfer gaps (stakes, an unfamiliar interviewer, no pause, an unfamiliar format), plans pressure mocks and a routine with recovery phrases, uses the log, and treats the bad round as data without spiraling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd first write down what happened within an hour: which question, what I felt, what I did, and how long it lasted. Then I'd look for what differed from my mocks. Typically: stakes (a real outcome); an unfamiliar interviewer with a neutral face; a format or tool I had not used; no ability to restart; and fatigue in a later round. I'd check each against my log.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then I'd change the practice. First, make mocks resemble the real thing more: a partner I don't know, the real tool, a stranger's neutral expression, no restarts, and a full loop with breaks to practice stamina. Second, build a routine and recovery moves: a slow first sentence, the plan stated aloud, and three phrases: 'Let me take a moment to structure this,' 'I want to correct something,' and 'Let me summarize where I am.' I'd rehearse them aloud until they are automatic. Third, practice the specific moment: have the partner stop me mid-answer with a hard question and practice recovering, since the freeze often comes from an unexpected interruption.</p>\n<p>&quot;And I'd treat the bad round as one data point. I'd reset between rounds (stand, water, one line on what to improve) and not replay it. After the loop I'd write what I'd change and fix one or two things. A freeze is common; what matters is whether the next attempt shows a better recovery.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Diagnosis; realistic practice; rehearsed recovery; resilience.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the nerves are severe and recurring? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then practice alone may not be enough. I'd talk to a doctor or a counselor, since persistent anxiety is something to treat, not just to practice around. In the meantime I'd use the routine and shorter rounds to build confidence.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Would you tell the interviewer you were nervous? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A brief, calm acknowledgment is fine if it helps me reset ('give me a second to organize my thoughts'). I wouldn't dwell on it or use it as an excuse; the recovery is what they see.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c34-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C34-05 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How do you know when you are ready for a loop?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;When I feel confident.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses mock scores and consistency.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sets measurable criteria (calibrated scores by dimension, timing, probe survival, full loops), uses outside scorers, recognizes that feeling is unreliable, and plans the taper.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Not by feeling, since fluency with the material isn't the same as performing under conditions (CS-C34-02). I'd set measurable criteria and check them. First, calibrated scores: an average of at least 3.5 across round types over the last six realistic mocks, with no dimension central to a round below 3. Second, consistency: the spread across mocks small, so that I'm not strong one day and weak the next. Third, timing: finishing each round type within about ten percent of its budget with the recommendation and risks included. Fourth, stories: each of my bank survives three levels of probing without invented detail, and no story repeats across rounds. Fifth, at least two full mock loops with breaks, scored by outside partners, with the last one at a similar level to the first and no sharp fade in later rounds. Sixth, logistics: the format, the policy, the tools and the time zone confirmed.</p>\n<p>&quot;If those hold, I'm as ready as practice can make me, and the remaining step is rest. If a criterion fails, that tells me what to work on and whether to move the date, which is sometimes possible and worth asking the recruiter about early. Readiness is also not perfection: no one is at a 5 on everything, and waiting for it means never interviewing.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Measurable criteria; outside scoring; consistency; honesty about feeling.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if you're at 3.2, not 3.5, and the loop is next week? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd go, with a plan: put the final days on the weakest dimension, rehearse the recovery moves and protect sleep. A loop is also practice: the data from the real questions are valuable. I wouldn't cancel unless a critical dimension is below 2.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you account for partners who score generously? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd calibrate, as in Worked example 4: compare partners with each other and with my own scoring of the same recordings, and use the stricter, better-evidenced scores. If everyone is generous, I'd ask one experienced person to score two mocks.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c34-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C34-06 · Hard · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you use an AI tool to prepare for interviews without fooling yourself?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd ask it to give me questions and feedback.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the risk of flattery and inaccuracy.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses a rubric, quoted evidence, two runs, calibration against a human with a bias figure, verification of facts, a division of use (prompts and critique, not final scores), live human mocks, and a rule against use in real interviews unless permitted.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd use it for what it does well and check it for what it does badly. It is good for generating prompts by round type, role-playing an interviewer with probes, summarizing my answer to show where the structure broke, and suggesting follow-ups. It is poor at scoring honestly: models tend to flatter, to be inconsistent, to prefer long fluent answers and to invent facts about companies.</p>\n<p>&quot;So I'd have a routine (FW-C34-08). I'd give it the scorecard and ask for a score with a quote from my answer for each dimension; if it can't quote me, I discard the score. I'd ask for the strongest criticism first. I'd run it twice and compare: big differences mean noise. I'd calibrate it against a human: I'd have a partner and the tool score the same five answers. Suppose the tool gives 9, 8 and 9 where the partner gives 6, 5 and 6: the tool's bias is +3.</p>\n<p>Then I'd treat its scores as meaningless and use it only for prompts and critique, or subtract the bias if it is steady. I'd verify every fact about a company or a process against primary sources. I'd keep live mocks with people at least weekly, since the tool applies no real pressure. I wouldn't paste confidential material from an employer. And I'd follow each company's policy: some say you may not use AI tools in the interview, and I'd comply.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Division of labor; evidence quotes; a bias calculation; verification; compliance.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the tool's feedback is more detailed than a friend's? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Detail isn't accuracy. I'd check a sample of its critiques against the transcript and the scorecard; if its observations are right, I'd use it more for critique, while still using people for scoring and pressure.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Could using an AI tool in preparation hurt you? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It could, by making me comfortable with fluent, polished answers that sound rehearsed, or by teaching me wrong facts. I'd guard against both by practicing aloud without it, keeping my stories true and in my own words, and checking facts.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c34-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C34-07 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your self-scores are consistently higher than your partner's. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd try to be more critical of myself.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes the gap and checks the evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes bias and error, finds the dimension-level pattern, uses anchors and quoted evidence, corrects the scores, rechecks after a few mocks and uses the partner's scores for decisions meanwhile.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd measure it. Over eight mocks, suppose my scores are 4, 4, 3, 5, 4, 3, 4, 5 and my partner's are 3, 3, 3, 4, 3, 3, 3, 4. The differences are +1, +1, 0, +1, +1, 0, +1, +1: a bias of +0.75 and an error of 0.75. That's consistent, so it can be corrected: I'd treat my 4 as about a 3.25. Then I'd find why: where we differ, I'd compare the evidence. Maybe I credit myself for naming a framework while the partner scores whether it fit the problem; maybe I credit a metric for being named while they want a numerator, denominator and guardrail. That tells me what my scale lacks. I'd fix it with the written anchors (reread before scoring) and by quoting the sentence that supports each score.</p>\n<p>&quot;Meanwhile I'd use the partner's scores to choose what to practice, since mine are inflated. After three more mocks with anchors and quotes, I'd recompute: if the bias falls to about +0.3, the correction is working. If it does not, I'd get a second scorer, in case my partner is the unusual one.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Measurement; a pattern; a mechanism; a recheck.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if your partner is the one who is too harsh? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd check with a second scorer and with the anchors. If two scorers agree and the anchors support them, the harsh partner is right; if the second agrees with me, I'd tell my partner what evidence I see for the higher score and adjust the anchors together.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Is overrating always bad? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Mild overrating can help confidence, and it hurts decisions: you practice the wrong things. I want my scores to be accurate and my confidence to come from the data.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c34-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C34-08 · Hard · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design a mock-interview program for a cohort of 30 aspiring product managers.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Pair them up and have them do mock interviews.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines users, problems and a structure with scoring.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Identifies the binding constraint (interviewer supply and feedback quality), designs pairing and swapping, scorecards and calibration sessions, uses AI for solo practice with checks, sizes the load, and measures outcomes with guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Users: the 30 candidates (and volunteer experienced PMs as optional coaches). The problem I'd focus on: not enough good practice with reliable feedback; the binding constraint is interviewer supply and feedback quality, not motivation. Evidence I'd gather first: how many mocks each person has done, how their self-scores compare with outside scores, and which round types they lack practice in.</p>\n<p>&quot;Design. <em>Structure:</em> each candidate does two partner mocks a week, swapping roles so that everyone interviews as well as is interviewed, which solves supply: 30 candidates × 2 mocks = 60 mocks a week, each about an hour, so 60 candidate hours and 60 interviewer hours, which the swap covers. <em>Quality:</em> a shared scorecard with anchors for each round type; a short training session where everyone scores the same recorded mock and we compare, to calibrate the group; a coach (an experienced PM) joins one mock a month per pair. <em>Solo practice:</em> an AI tool for prompts and critique, with the checking routine, and a rule that live mocks come first. <em>Rotation:</em> partners rotate every two weeks, so no one is tuned to one style. <em>Data:</em> each person's calibrated scores by dimension and an error log, reviewed every two weeks; the weakest dimension for each gets targeted drills.</p>\n<p>&quot;Metrics: mean calibrated score by dimension at the start and the end; the bias of self-scores; timing adherence; the share of participants completing at least 10 scored mocks; and, over a longer period, interview progression. Guardrails: participant stress and drop-out, and the burden on volunteer coaches. I'd launch with a pilot of eight people for three weeks, check whether calibration sessions reduce bias, and expand.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> The constraint; a self-sustaining design; calibration; AI with checks; measurement.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the group's average skill is low and feedback is blunt or wrong? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Calibration sessions and anchors are the answer: everyone scores the same example, we discuss differences, and coaches model evidence-based feedback. I'd also use a rule: feedback must quote evidence and give one fix, which prevents vague or harsh comments.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you tell whether the program works? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Compare calibrated scores before and after for participants and for a similar group that did not join, if there is one; track bias and consistency; ask for self-reported readiness but weigh it below scores; and wait for outcomes over months with the caveat that interview results depend on many things.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c34-09\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C34-09 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Walk me through the final week before a loop.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd review everything again and practice a lot.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A taper with light review and logistics.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A day-by-day plan with a final full mock at day minus seven, targeted drills, no new material in the last three days, logistics settled two days before, and rest, with a day-of routine and an after protocol.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Day minus seven: a last full mock loop, scored, and choose the one or two fixes that matter most. Minus six: drill those fixes; update the error log; confirm in writing the times, the time zone, the tools and the policy on assistance. Minus five: review the company brief and my 'why us'; say my tailored stories aloud. Minus four: two short drills on the weakest dimension; no new frameworks. Minus three: one partner mock of a single round type.</p>\n<p>Minus two: light review of story headlines, the brief and my questions; a tech check on the real device and network; stop new material. Minus one: rest: a short walk, one page of review, early sleep; prepare clothes, water, notes. Day zero: wake early, eat, light movement, a fifteen-minute review of headlines, a final tech check and a breath before each round; between rounds, reset. After: notes within an hour, a thank-you within a day, and rest.</p>\n<p>&quot;What I avoid: cramming new material, which raises anxiety and doesn't stick; a full mock the day before, which tires me; and unconfirmed logistics. If the loop is at an awkward hour in my time zone, I adjust sleep earlier in the week.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> A taper; logistics; routines; discipline about what not to do.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if you find a big gap on day minus three? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd make one targeted fix, not a rebuild: a drill and a retest on a different prompt, and an explicit plan for how I'll handle the gap in the round (for example, a clarifying question or a stated assumption). I'd accept some risk rather than disrupt my sleep.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle the last night's anxiety? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A short walk, a fixed stopping time for review, writing tomorrow's plan on one page, and the routine I rehearsed. If I can't sleep, I rest anyway; a bad night before a round is less damaging than worrying about it.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c34-10\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C34-10 · Hard · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you evaluate an AI tool that scores mock interviews before recommending it to others?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd try it and see if the feedback seems good.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares its scores with human scores.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Builds an eval: a sample of real answers scored by several experts, agreement among the experts, the tool's bias, error and consistency against their consensus, evidence quality, fairness across accents and styles, a pass bar, and a rollout with monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd treat it as an AI feature and build an eval (C25). First, a set of answers: about 250, from real mocks across round types and levels, with consent. Sizing: to estimate the tool's agreement with the human consensus within ±5 points at about 80% agreement needs 1.96² × 0.8 × 0.2 ÷ 0.05² ≈ 246 cases. Second, human scores: three experienced interviewers score each answer independently with the same scorecard; I'd compute their agreement beyond chance (kappa) and fix the anchors until it is acceptable, since a tool can't be better than its reference. Third, the tool's performance: bias (mean tool minus consensus), error (mean absolute difference), the share within one point, and consistency (two runs of the same answer). Fourth, evidence: does it quote the answer for each score? I'd sample fifty and check that the quotes exist and support the score. Fifth, fairness: compare bias by accent, speaking style, language of origin and answer length, since tools can penalize or reward style.</p>\n<p>&quot;Pass bar, for example: bias within ±0.3 points, error under 0.6, 85% within one point, consistency within one point on 90% of repeated runs, evidence quotes valid in at least 95% of samples, no group with bias differing by more than 0.3. If it passes, recommend it for critique and practice scoring with the caveat to confirm with a human each week, monitor on new answers monthly, and publish its measured limits.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> A sized eval; reference reliability; bias, error and consistency; evidence validity; fairness; a pass bar.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the tool's average is right but it is wrong on individual answers? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then it is fine for tracking trends over many answers and not for judging a single one. I'd say so in the recommendation, and show the error distribution, not just the mean.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the experts disagree with each other? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then the ground truth is uncertain, and I'd treat tool-human disagreement within the experts' own disagreement as acceptable. I'd improve the anchors and re-score, and report the tool's agreement relative to expert-to-expert agreement.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c34-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Every figure is invented.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c34-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C34-01 · Build a four-week schedule</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You have eight hours a week for four weeks before a loop of four rounds (product design, analytics, technical, behavioral), with analytics your weakest. Build a schedule (FW-C34-01).</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Preparation planning.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C34-01 and FW-C33-08.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Week one (8 hours): loop map and recruiter questions (1); three solo drills (3: metric definition, diagnose a change, structure); one partner mock on analytics (1.5); scoring and log (1); story bank notes (1.5). Week two: two partner mocks (3: analytics, behavioral), recorded review (1.5), drills on the log's top fix (2), technical reading with one drill (1.5). Week three: weakest-dimension diagnosis (1); targeted drills (3); one full mock loop of four rounds with breaks (3); company brief (1). Week four (tapering to about 4 hours): day minus seven last mock (2); drills on two fixes (1.5); logistics (0.5); taper from day minus four.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> If partners are scarce, replace one partner mock a week with a recorded solo mock and score it against the scorecard with quoted evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> More partner time gives better feedback; the schedule fits the hours available.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c34-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C34-02 · Score a transcript</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Score this answer (analytical round) on the scorecard in C34.2 (structure, metric choice, quantitative reasoning, experiment, recommendation), with evidence: &quot;Signups dropped 10%. I'd look at lots of things, like the website and ads and maybe a bug. I'd check the data and see what changed. It might be seasonal. Then I'd run an A/B test to fix it. We should improve the signup page.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Scoring practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> The transcript.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C34-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Structure 2 (&quot;lists unprioritized causes; no tree&quot;); metric choice 1 (&quot;signups not defined; no denominator or window&quot;); quantitative 1 (&quot;no numbers or segments&quot;); experiment 2 (&quot;'run an A/B test' with no unit, duration or what it tests&quot;); recommendation 2 (&quot;'improve the page' is not tied to a finding&quot;). Overall: no hire. To reach Strong: confirm the data, define signups (new accounts per unique visitor, daily), segment by platform and channel, list falsifiable hypotheses with the cheapest check first, and recommend based on the finding.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Score it, then rewrite it to a 4 and rescore.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Rewriting teaches what the anchors mean.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c34-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C34-03 · Calibrate</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Six mocks: self-scores 3, 4, 4, 3, 5, 4; partner scores 3, 3, 4, 2, 4, 3. Compute bias, error, the share within one point and the share exact; interpret.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C34-03 and Worked example 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Differences: 0, +1, 0, +1, +1, +1. Bias = 4 ÷ 6 = +0.67. Error = 0.67 (all non-negative). Within one point: 6 of 6 (100%). Exact: 2 of 6 (33%). Interpretation: consistent overrating of about two-thirds of a point; correct self-scores by subtracting about 0.67 until the bias shrinks, and find out by dimension which credit is unearned.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add dimension-level scores to see whether the gap is concentrated in one dimension.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> More mocks make the estimate of bias more reliable; six is the minimum.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c34-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C34-04 · Write evidence statements</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> From this answer, write four evidence statements an interviewer could record: &quot;I'd focus on busy parents planning a trip, because they coordinate everyone. The problem is collecting preferences without a long group chat. I'd test a voting board, measuring the share of trips where at least three members vote, with time-to-itinerary as a guardrail. If voting lowers planning speed I'd drop it.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The scorer's view.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> The transcript.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C33-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (1) <em>Chose a specific user with a reason</em> (busy parents who coordinate). (2) <em>Named a concrete problem</em> (collecting preferences without a long chat). (3) <em>Defined a primary metric and a guardrail</em> (voting share and time-to-itinerary). (4) <em>Stated a decision rule</em> (drop it if voting lowers planning speed). Gaps to note: no alternatives considered; no sizing of the problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a fifth: <em>what would be missing for Standout</em> (alternatives, a number).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Writing evidence statements teaches you to say quotable things.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c34-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C34-05 · Design a drill</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Your log shows that you ramble: answers run five minutes with the point at minute three. Design a drill (FW-C34-05) with a run instruction and a success test.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Targeted practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C34-05 and C32.1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Drill: answer first in 60 seconds.</em> Run: take any prompt; set a 60-second timer; give the answer and three named supports; record; replay. Repeat on five prompts, then extend each to two minutes, adding evidence while keeping the answer first. <em>Success test:</em> the answer appears within the first 15 seconds in at least four of five recordings; three supports are named up front; the answer fits in the time. <em>Retest:</em> on a different prompt after 72 hours, in a partner mock, counting the second at which the answer arrives.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a partner who raises a hand at 30 seconds if the answer has not appeared.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A strict clock feels artificial and builds the habit.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c34-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C34-06 · Diagnose and allocate</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Four calibrated mocks, averages: structure 3.25, metrics 2.25, user 4.25, communication 2.75. Importance: structure 3, metrics 3, user 2, communication 2. You have 16 hours. Compute importance × gap and the hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Targeted practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Gap = 5 − average.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C34-06 and Worked example 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Importance × gap: structure 3 × 1.75 = 5.25; metrics 3 × 2.75 = 8.25; user 2 × 0.75 = 1.5; communication 2 × 2.25 = 4.5; total 19.5. Hours: 16 × 5.25 ÷ 19.5 = 4.3; metrics 6.8; user 1.2; communication 3.7. About 4, 7, 1 and 4 hours. Metrics gets the most; user empathy, already a strength, gets maintenance only. Set a goal of metrics 2.25 to 3.5, retest in two weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a minimum of 2 hours to every dimension and scale the rest.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A formula guides allocation and judgment adjusts for veto risks.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c34-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C34-07 · Recovery scripts</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write, in your own words, a recovery phrase and a next action for three moments: you blank on a question; you realize you gave a wrong number a minute ago; you are lost in the middle of an answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Nerves and recovery.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C34-07.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Blank:</em> &quot;Let me take a moment to structure this.&quot; Then write two or three headings (user, problem, options) and begin with the first. <em>Wrong number:</em> &quot;I want to correct something I said: the figure is 20%, not 2%, so the conclusion changes to X.&quot; Then continue. <em>Lost:</em> &quot;Let me summarize where I am: I've framed the problem and chosen the user; I still need to give the solution and the metric.&quot; Then do those. Rehearse each aloud three times, until each takes five seconds to say and sounds calm.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a fourth: for an interruption you can't answer, &quot;I don't know that; here is how I would find out.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Rehearsed phrases free attention for the problem.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c34-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C34-08 · Check an AI score</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> An AI tool gives your three answers scores of 9, 8 and 9 out of 10, with no quotes. A partner gives 6, 5 and 6. Compute the tool's bias and say what you do.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Using AI to practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C34-08.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Differences: +3, +3, +3: bias +3 and error 3. The tool overrates by three points and gives no evidence. Actions: ask it for scores with a quoted sentence for each dimension and discard any that it can't quote; ask for the strongest criticism first; run it twice and compare; use it only for prompts and critique, and rely on the partner's scores; check with a larger sample (five answers) before trusting any correction; verify any fact it states against primary sources.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Subtract three points if the bias is steady over ten answers; do not do this with three.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A tool with a steady, known bias can still help track trends.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c34-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C34-09 · Final-week plan with a time-zone twist</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Your loop is at 9 a.m. Pacific time (UTC−8) on a day in winter, and you live in India (UTC+5:30). Convert the time and write a final-week plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Final week.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C34.10 and C33.13.</p>\n";
